text:
Use design tokens
The first section of the stylesheet defines reusable values. This keeps the site consistent and makes a redesign faster.
:root {
  --ink: #102a43;
  --muted: #52606d;
  --accent: #d97706;
  --paper: #f7f9fc;
  --radius: 18px;
}
Instead of repeating a color in many selectors, use var(--accent). The result is easier to read and maintain.

Build reusable components
The homepage uses classes such as .hero, .skill-card, and .btn. These classes separate content from presentation and let the same visual language appear across multiple pages.
.skill-card {
  padding: 1.35rem;
  border: 1px solid #d9e2ec;
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  background: white;
}

Make the layout responsive
CSS Grid handles the desktop layout, while a media query stacks the content on smaller screens:
@media (max-width: 720px) {
  .hero,
  .grid-3,
  .metrics {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}
This approach keeps the page readable on phones without duplicating the content.

8 8. Prompt: How would you adapt this analysis for the final CEP dataset?

8.1 Prompt
Use the data for your MSDM CEP and pick variables relevant to your Analytics Objectives.


8.2 Response
For the final CEP version, I would replace pizzaplace with the project’s actual data source, preserve the same reproducible wrangling-to-gt workflow, and choose grouped variables that map directly to the CEP questions. The key design choice would be to keep only the metrics that a stakeholder can act on or interpret.
A useful workflow would be:

identify the business question;
select the outcome and comparison variables;
group and summarise the data;
check that the descriptive statistics are correct;
build the gt table;
add the smallest visual element that improves interpretation; and
use the final table as an exhibit in the written report or presentation.
